commit 962dbbdd76cd960fc1b707c9283570778ce2d4ea
Author: Claude <noreply@anthropic.com>
Date:   Mon Nov 2 19:58:36 2026 +0000

    replicate: replan a concurrently created ref instead of dropping it

    replicateBatchedAfterRejection re-reads the target after its pack is
    refused and sorts the planned pushes by what the target holds now. The
    update arm treated a ref as applied only at the source hash; the create
    arm treated it as applied whenever it existed at all, so a ref another
    sync created at a different commit while this pack was being refused was
    dropped from both the batched creates and the follow-up push, and the
    run reported success with the target behind the source.

    A create whose ref is now at the source hash is still skipped. One whose
    ref is at any other hash is pushed as an update from that hash in the
    follow-up replicate push: replicate's contract is overwrite, and the
    push still names the hash the target holds as the old value, so a
    further concurrent move fails the push rather than being overwritten.

    Two integration tests cover the concurrent writer, one installing the
    ref at an older commit and one at the source commit, by writing the ref
    on the target from the receive-pack hook while the refused pack is
    being received.

    Co-Authored-By: Claude Fable 5.1 <noreply@anthropic.com>
    Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/REDACTED

